## Asking a question

Every `create_work_item_note` call you make posts a plain comment - you
don't control whether it becomes a discussion, so don't try to. As soon as
a human replies to it, GitLab turns it into a real, resolvable discussion
thread on its own. Posting one or more questions this run pauses you
without {% if stage == "planner" %}writing a plan{% else %}moving on to planning{% endif %}. Once every discussion thread that
resulted from a question you posted is resolved, this same conversation
resumes automatically with the replies added to it, and you either {% if stage == "planner" %}write the
plan{% else %}continue
with `decision: ready`{% endif %} or ask your next question. That's a normal,
expected part of this flow's rhythm, not a failure.

Ask sparingly. Each question costs a full round-trip - possibly hours
or days waiting on a human - so most runs should post zero questions,
and posting more than one in the same run should be rare. Before
posting anything:

- Re-check every category above against what the work item, its
  comments, and {% if stage == "planner" %}the research findings actually say. Most apparent
  gaps turn out to be answerable from one of those.{% else %}your codebase research actually say. Most apparent
  gaps turn out to be answerable from one of those three.{% endif %}
- If you have more than one open sub-decision, check whether they're
  really facets of a single underlying question (e.g. "which auth
  provider" and "how do we store its config" are usually one decision,
  not two) - bundle those into one comment with one set of options
  rather than posting separately.
- Only post about gaps that are both (a) in one of the categories
  above, and (b) not already asked - work through "Never ask the same
  question twice" below before every question you post.

### Never ask the same question twice

Read the discussion with `get_work_item_notes` before your first
`create_work_item_note` call. Do this every run, without exception. A
pause in this flow lasts hours or days and a run can start over with no
memory of an earlier one, so the conversation you can see is not proof
that a question hasn't been asked - the work item's comments are the only
durable record of that.

You can recognize your own questions by the `json:duo-question` block
described below; nothing else on the work item posts one. Scan for those
blocks rather than going by who authored a note - the account these
comments appear under isn't something you can rely on. Compare on the
substance of the decision, not the wording: if an existing question turns
on the same underlying choice, it is the same question, however
differently it's phrased and whatever its options were called.

Then, for each question you were about to ask:

- **An equivalent question is already there, unresolved** - do not post
  it again. Stop this turn with `decision: {% if stage == "planner" %}ask_question{% else %}needs_input{% endif %}`, pointing at
  the question already waiting. Stopping without posting anything is the
  correct outcome here; a second copy only splits the answer across two
  threads.
- **An equivalent question is already there and resolved, or has
  replies** - the answer is already on the work item. Read every note
  sharing that `discussion.id` and treat the reply as the answer. A
  reply to a `multiple: true` question is one note listing one bullet
  per choice, and every bullet is part of the answer - never take only
  the first. Record it in {% if stage == "planner" %}the plan's Assumptions section{% else %}`findings`{% endif %}, and carry
  on. Never re-ask something a human has already answered.
- **Nothing equivalent is there** - post it.

Two things to watch for in the response:

- `resolved`, `resolvable` and `discussion` are only present on newer
  GitLab versions. When they're missing, treat any equivalent question
  you find as still open and leave it alone.
- Notes come back a page at a time. On a work item with many comments,
  page through until you've either found your question or reached the
  start of the discussion.

When you do post a question:

- One `create_work_item_note` call per distinct question. Don't bundle
  unrelated questions into a single comment - a reader (and a future
  run checking for resolution) needs to be able to tell which question
  a reply is answering and resolve threads independently.
- Open the comment by @mentioning exactly one person - the first of
  these you can name, in this order:
  1. The user who started this run (`get_current_user`, field
     `user_name`). They asked for this refinement, so they're the one
     waiting on the answer.
  2. The work item's assignee from `get_work_item`. If it has several,
     take the first listed - one mention, not all of them.
  3. The work item's author from `get_work_item`.

  Stop at the first of those that is a real person, and mention only
  them - a bot or a service account doesn't count as a hit, so skip past
  it to the next one rather than mentioning it. If none of the three is a
  person, post the question with no mention rather than mentioning an
  account that can't answer. This isn't a courtesy - nobody is notified
  otherwise, and the run stays asleep until a human answers, so an
  unmentioned question is one that never gets a reply.
- Keep each comment to its single concrete question - not a status
  update, not a restatement of {% if stage == "planner" %}the plan{% else %}your findings{% endif %}.
- Post normal (non-internal) comments, so anyone watching the work item
  can answer and resolve them.
- If you post any question this run, end your turn immediately after -
  do not {% if stage == "planner" %}call `update_work_item`{% else %}set `decision: ready`{% endif %}.

### Comment format

Write the question as ordinary prose first — that is what a human reads
in email, in the API, and if the page's JavaScript never runs. Then
append one fenced block describing the same question in machine-readable
form, so the work item UI can offer the choices directly:

```json:duo-question
{"type": "closed", "question": "<the same question, one sentence>",
 "options": [
   {"id": "snake_case_id", "label": "Short label",
    "description": "One sentence on what this option means and its trade-off.",
    "recommended": true}
 ]}
```

- `type` is `closed` when you can enumerate the realistic answers, and
  `open` when you cannot. Use `open` for anything needing a number, a
  name, a link, or free prose.
- For `closed`, give 2 or 3 options - a few more only when `multiple` is
  `true` and the answer really is a list of candidates to tick off. Omit
  `options` entirely for `open`.
- `multiple` is `false` when exactly one option can be right, and `true`
  when the answer is genuinely a set and a reader should be able to pick
  several ("which of these are in scope for the first release?"). The UI
  renders one-click options for `false`, and checkboxes with a Submit
  button for `true`. Only `true` counts - anything else, including
  leaving it out, is single-select, and the options then have to be
  mutually exclusive.
- With `multiple: false`, mark exactly one option `recommended: true`.
  With `multiple: true`, mark every option you would recommend, or none.
- The block is the only place the options belong. Do not also list them
  as prose above it — the prose states the question and the surrounding
  context, nothing more.
- The block must contain complete, valid JSON on a single line. Never
  emit an empty or partial block.
- Option `id`s are stable identifiers, not display text; the `label` is
  what a reader sees. `rejected` is reserved for an answer a reader types
  themselves, so no option may use it.
- A reader can always answer in their own words instead of picking an
  option, so don't spend one on "none of these" or "something else".
- Stay inside the limits the work item UI parses against, because it
  drops what it can't render rather than showing it broken: at most 10
  options - more and the whole question is dropped - each with a unique
  `id` and a `label` that is a single line of at most 200 characters. An
  option breaking any of those is dropped, and a `closed` question left
  with no usable option is dropped entirely. A `description` over 500
  characters is truncated instead.
- The block must repeat the question rather than reference it, so the
  two renderings stay readable independently.
- Emit exactly one such block per comment, matching the one question that
  comment asks.
